lesson.body:
GAAR is in **Chapter XI** of the Income-tax Act, 2025, sections 178 onwards (corresponds to sections 95 to 102 of the 1961 Act):
- **Section 178**: applicability; GAAR may be invoked notwithstanding anything in the Act, and the arrangement may be declared an impermissible avoidance arrangement (IAA).
- **Section 179**: an IAA is an arrangement whose **main purpose is to obtain a tax benefit** and which has at least one **tainted element**:
  - creates rights or obligations not ordinarily created between persons dealing at arm's length;
  - results in misuse or abuse of the provisions of the Act;
  - lacks commercial substance or is deemed to lack it (in whole or part);
  - is entered into or carried out by means or in a manner not ordinarily employed for bona fide purposes.
- **Section 180**: arrangements deemed to lack commercial substance (round trip financing, accommodating party, elements that offset each other, disguising location or ownership).
- **Section 181**: consequences: disregarding or recharacterising the arrangement, disregarding an accommodating party, treating parties as one, reallocating income and expenditure, relocating residence or situs, denying treaty benefits, and recharacterising equity as debt and the reverse.

An arrangement is presumed to have the main purpose of obtaining a tax benefit if a step or part has that purpose, even if the whole does not. Section 159(6) makes Chapter XI apply even where a tax treaty is more beneficial.

Transfer pricing is in **sections 161 to 173** of the Income-tax Act, 2025 (corresponds to sections 92 to 92F of the 1961 Act). Section 161 requires income from an international transaction or specified domestic transaction to be computed having regard to the **arm's length price (ALP)**; section 165 deals with determination of ALP.

**Associated enterprises** (corresponds to s.92A): one enterprise participates in the management, control or capital of the other, directly or indirectly. Deemed AE tests include:
- holding 26% or more of voting power;
- a loan of 51% or more of the book value of total assets of the borrower;
- guarantee of 10% or more of total borrowings;
- appointing more than half the board or one or more executive directors;
- complete dependence on the other for know-how, patents or similar for manufacture;
- 90% or more of raw materials supplied by the other (or persons specified by it) at prices it influences.

**International transaction** (corresponds to s.92B): a transaction between two or more AEs, at least one a non-resident, in tangible or intangible property, services, lending or borrowing, cost sharing, or business restructuring. A transaction with an unrelated person is deemed to be with an AE if the terms are determined in substance by the AE.

**Specified domestic transaction** (corresponds to s.92BA): certain domestic transactions (such as with related parties of a business claiming profit-linked deductions) where the aggregate exceeds **₹20 crore**.
